00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.760
So it's a very interesting
social meets finance

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.160
kind of a thing.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.130
And StockTwits-- this is an
example of one of the biggest

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.174
social finance platforms.

00:06:11.174 --> 00:06:13.090
These are the guys that
invented the cash tag.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.450
So the point I'm
trying to make here

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.280
is that these are
examples of startups

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.380
that had this great
idea for financial help,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.900
and now all they really need
is the market data to power it.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:30.340
And so it should be
pretty simple and it is.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:33.270
So if you go to the traditional
providers-- the Thomson

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.770
Reuters, the
Bloombergs, the IDCs,

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.460
you first pay a
hefty licensing fee.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.470
Then you start to
build your data feed

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.350
handlers against legacy
proprietary protocols built

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:44.820
in the 70s.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:47.606
You put your servers out
in colocations in New York

00:06:47.606 --> 00:06:49.980
and Chicago to be closer to
the exchanges and [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:50.950
plants.

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:53.130
You've got to build out
your own production database

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.213
infrastructure to store
the data that you actually

00:06:55.213 --> 00:06:56.116
have to pull down.

00:06:56.116 --> 00:06:57.990
And how you build this
service infrastructure

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.600
that actually serves the
data out to your applications

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.430
just so your iPhone app can show
the current price of Google.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:08.490
We think that's kind of crazy,
especially because all of this

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:12.380
is not a differentiator
for these companies.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:14.520
If you're a robo
advisor, if you're

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.200
doing a social media platform,
you just need the data.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:19.950
You don't want to spend that
much time and money doing

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:21.780
something that's
really a commodity.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.060
So this is where
Xignite comes in.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:28.110
We offer what we refer to as a
zero footprint market data API.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:31.790
So basically what that means is
zero production infrastructure

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:34.560
to get financial market
data into your route.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:37.080
We have REST APIs, which
is basically the same thing

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:38.910
as typing a URL into a browser.

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.050
That's how easy it is.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:44.010
We return data in XML or
JSON, which any application

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:49.170
developer can easily bring into
a mobile app or their websites.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.860
We offer data across all asset
classes, currencies, metals,

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.860
commodities, equities,
options, indices.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.119
We have real-time
data, historical data,

00:07:58.119 --> 00:07:59.910
company fundamentals--
basically everything

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.870
you need for a
financial application.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:07.910
In addition to these
request response APIs,

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:10.520
we also have other
ways to get the data.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:14.330
We can stream the data
directly to your device.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:18.770
We have the ability to let you
define market conditions that

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:19.865
notify you when it occurs.

00:08:19.865 --> 00:08:21.990
Maybe your favorite stock
hits a certain threshold.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.940
You want email or such.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.900
We have HTML 5 widgets that
are predefined, prepackaged,

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.960
and customizable, which you
can directly put into your app.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:32.870
Once again, zero footprint-- you
don't have to host a widgets.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.039
We host them.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:38.570
We manage the checking of all
the conditions on our side.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:40.239
Cloud streaming-- same thine.

00:08:40.239 --> 00:08:41.780
All the infrastructure
is on our side

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:43.738
so you don't have to put
anything on your side.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:48.910
So which brings us
to why am I here?

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:50.980
Well, we do have one
area which we have,

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:54.340
quite honestly, a very
suboptimal solution for,

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:58.370
which is basically our customers
who want to do analytics.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.130
They have certain
algorithms to try

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:01.930
to predict the way
the market's going

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:04.570
to go with different data.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:06.770
They want to do backtesting
based on the historicals

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.190
to get a feel for how
accurate their stuff would

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:10.880
have happened in the past.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:14.020
So today, we have
the opportunity

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:18.010
to download bulk CSVs, which
people still use today.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:22.540
We have historical options
data, index data, equities data.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:25.000
We even have all the
trades and quotes

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.300
for US exchange for
the past 10 years.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.230
That's a ton of data, which is
just terabytes and terabytes

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:32.320
of data-- well, I
forget I'm at Google--

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:33.600
it's only terabytes of data.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.380
It's not petabytes of data.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:37.387
But still, that's a
ton of time, which

00:09:37.387 --> 00:09:39.220
quite honestly, the
easy way to get the data

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:40.892
is sort of what
Matt referred to--

00:09:40.892 --> 00:09:42.850
it's actually getting a
hard disk with the data

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:43.808
and shipped it to them.

00:09:43.808 --> 00:09:46.670
It's much faster than trying
to bring it over the internet.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.166
So what I'd like to
do now is show you

00:09:49.166 --> 00:09:50.290
what we've been working on.

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:52.150
It's basically taking
our historical data

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.400
and bringing it into Big
Query and making it available

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:58.260
directly from the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:01.720
Can we switch the screens?

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.280
OK.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:08.640
So this first screen here is
basically Google BigQuery.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.510
We have tables of historical
data for currencies, equities,

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:14.700
