00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.970
So today we're going
to talk a little bit

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:29.550
about residential real estate.

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:33.660
Residential real estate in the
United States is a $30 trillion

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:34.460
market.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.960
It's the largest asset
class in the United States

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:38.910
or the world for that matter.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:42.440
And whether you're a major hedge
fund, you're a big investor,

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:47.130
you're a big lender, or
even just a homeowner,

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:50.530
we all struggle with two
fundamental questions.

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:53.910
Number one, what is the
value of this property?

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:56.520
And number two, is the
price going to go up

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:58.369
or is it going to go down?

00:13:58.369 --> 00:13:59.910
And at HouseCanary,
that's what we've

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:02.460
been focused on
solving and providing

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.790
to a number of sophisticated
investors and lenders

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:08.570
over the last couple of
years using massive data

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:11.490
sets and a lot of machine
learning that I know

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:13.390
is being talked about a lot.

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:15.870
And we're excited to
put a couple of parts

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:19.050
of our platform on the
Google Cloud platform today.

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:27.320
So let's talk about the state
of affairs in real estate today.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.140
It's massively fragmented.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:31.840
In essence, you have
$120 million properties

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:34.690
in the United States
every property

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:36.340
is its own unique snowflake.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:37.690
They're all different.

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:41.350
And underlying that, the
data is super fragmented.

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:45.020
You've got 3,000 or 3,200
different county assessors

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:48.610
and public recorders, 750
different multiple listing

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:51.760
services, a number
of other sources.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:53.950
The problem is is
every one of those data

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:55.300
sources are all different.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:57.620
There's no common
data standards.

00:14:57.620 --> 00:14:59.740
So the information that
you get from one never

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:00.730
aligns with the next.

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:05.850
The definitions of what it
means are all different.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:09.000
And in many cases, it's
all still paper-based.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.844
That's what's slowing down
the process of buying homes,

00:15:11.844 --> 00:15:12.510
lending on them.

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:17.970
So what we've been
really focused on

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:21.360
is really bringing this
information together

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.440
about this massive asset class,
bringing in the 21st century,

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.960
and largely indexing
all the details

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.360
about every home,
every mortgage,

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:31.980
and every neighborhood.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:33.960
And when you do that,
there's three things

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:35.550
that this really
starts to enable.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.430
Number one, we can
get deeply detailed.

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.920
Today, if you read
the newspaper,

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:46.330
it'll come out that Kay Schiller
says that Chicago is up by 4%.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.540
But what does that mean
about my local area?

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:51.660
For us, we're now providing
that level of detail

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:57.600
by every 381 US metropolitan
areas, 18,000 zip codes--

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:01.120
every zip code where there's a
population of 1,000 or more--

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:03.780
and now blocks,
three million blocks.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:07.360
How are prices changing--
getting down to the details.

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:11.580
The second area is
really being predictive.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.170
Past is not prologue
for the future.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:15.930
I think we've all realized
that through what's

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:17.430
happened in the
markets over time.

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:21.120
And the idea is to
bring the history

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:23.100
but then also use
machine learning

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:25.440
and predictive analytics
to really forecast

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.080
what's going to happen
in the markets using data

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.760
that no single human
could ever figure out.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:32.800
It's too complicated
at this point.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.820
And finally just bringing
the amount of data

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:39.120
that you can bear and
building highly accurate

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.610
values for 90 million
homes that are updated

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:45.150
monthly, rental values
for 60 million homes,

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:48.330
given how large single
family rental is now

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:49.320
as an asset class.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:55.620
So to make this real, what we
wanted to do and what we did--

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:57.960
it was a really easy
process for us--

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:02.280
is we loaded about a billion
data points from HouseCanary,

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:05.130
from our platform,
into BigQuery.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:08.730
And then we basically
put on top Tableau.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:11.910
So it's just fully stock
what we're going to show--

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:13.700
just pulling some examples out.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:16.050
So given that we're
in San Francisco,

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:19.530
I wanted to start with
an example around prices.

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:21.540
What's happened with
prices in San Francisco

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:24.329
over the last year?

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:28.880
Most people would say, well,
prices changed by about 9%

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:31.170
right in this area.

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:34.590
But if you look at
it, on the low end,

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:36.930
you've got Pacific
Heights in red

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:39.074
that basically grew by 7.1%.

00:17:39.074 --> 00:17:40.740
You've got the Mission
a couple of miles

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:46.330
away at a zip code level
that grew at 13.6%.

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:49.270
And if you go out to the East
Bay and parts of Oakland,

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:51.230
it's even grown by a
lot more than that.

00:17:56.320 --> 00:18:03.370
But now, the idea is to not
only deal at the zip code level,

00:18:03.370 --> 00:18:06.820
but actually to then
dig in block by block.

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:08.540
That's where
investments are made.

00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:10.600
That's where money
is made and lost.

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:14.350
And so for us, dealing
now at the block level,

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:18.310
we dig into these zip codes
and provide this information.

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:21.359
There's these nice
parent-child relationships

00:18:21.359 --> 00:18:22.900
that we've linked
everything together

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:26.740
so I can look at multiple
levels and now we're

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:30.130
down at the block level,
looking into the future.

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:32.950
And basically, I
can see that there's

00:18:32.950 --> 00:18:37.090
parts of San Francisco
that are likely to decline

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:40.210
and are declining right
now at a block level--

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:41.320
parts of the Presidio.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:45.820
And you go a few miles away,
and prices are going up--

00:18:45.820 --> 00:18:47.230
double digits.

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:49.510
And for us, that's the
dispersion of returns,

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:51.550
getting down to
the segmentation.

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:53.560
That's where the
information's really available

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:57.790
and the opportunities for making
better investment and lending

00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:58.440
decisions.

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:03.860
So now, let's actually
think about how

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:07.820
we might actually take that one
more level down to properties.

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:09.830
For HouseCanary, what
we've done and what

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:14.960
we've put up into BigQuery
and GCP is basically

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:19.490
for every single property,
we have a unique identifier

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:23.300
linked to the address, linked
to the latitude and longitude.

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:26.870
So now you can start actually
looking at every property,

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:28.550
not only against
HouseCanary data,

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:31.490
but linking it to
your own databases,

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:34.490
linking it out to
other data sets

00:19:34.490 --> 00:19:37.920
that we'll talk about,
where latitude and longitude

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:39.920
and joins are really important.

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:42.560
And you can start
identifying opportunities.

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:44.900
In this case, we just
pulled up two properties

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:47.930
sold at about the same
time, look very similar.

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:50.730
What's going to happen
over the next year?

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:52.940
One's likely to
have price declines,

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:58.190
the other is likely to
have price increases.

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:02.150
But this isn't really all about
doing this with two properties.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.490
This is about lots
of properties.

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:09.750
And so whether you're a major
lender, a major investor,

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:12.950
a retailer, this is
where the use case really

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:16.730
comes in for how to use
this kind of information.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:20.240
So maybe I'm a major lender
and I'm thinking about--

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:24.080
I hold loans in the
San Francisco area.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:26.060
For those loans
that I have, which

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:28.910
ones are going to
see increases, which

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:30.400
ones are going to see declines?

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:31.550
Where is there opportunity?

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:32.970
Where is there risk?

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:34.970
How do I change my
lending practices?

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:38.810
How do I manage
risk ahead of time?

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:42.080
I might also be an investor
that's not comparing

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:43.430
to my current portfolio.

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:46.910
But I might be looking for
new investment opportunities,

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:49.400
and you can start
to think about how

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:51.590
I start to look at
investment opportunities that

00:20:51.590 --> 00:20:57.050
may be coming up through
different bankruptcies,

00:20:57.050 --> 00:21:00.470
through MLS, linking
it to our evaluations,

00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:03.590
our rental analytics
if I'm looking to rent,

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:05.540
and thinking about where
prices are starting

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:07.550
to increase or decrease.

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:10.820
Many, many opportunities
to basically search--

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:12.345
find opportunities.

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:21.090
So on GCP, we've
basically loaded--

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:22.830
as I mentioned--
about a billion data

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:26.280
points to begin off
of our platform.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:28.650
There's a lot more where
this came from, and based

00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:34.500
on interest and where the
interest and everybody guides

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:36.762
us, we'll continue to add more.

00:21:36.762 --> 00:21:38.220
But really what
we've done and what

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:42.420
we started with was basically
adding first home price

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:43.530
indices--

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:48.030
home price indices for
all US metro areas,

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:53.090
18,000 zip codes that cover
about 95% of the US population,

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:54.970
and 3 million blocks.

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:56.700
And for each of
those, we've basically

00:21:56.700 --> 00:22:02.230
provided historical monthly
updates for the last 40 years.

00:22:02.230 --> 00:22:07.170
So you can see about three
economic cycles and forecasts.

00:22:07.170 --> 00:22:12.690
We've also included our
valuations that are third-party

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:14.970
reviewed and ranked
as the most accurate

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:17.910
in the market for
90 million homes.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:20.070
And then finally,
our rental values

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:22.320
on 60 million
homes that are used

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:25.290
by some of the big
household names

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:28.430
in the big institutional
investors, who have gone out,

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:31.980
bought a lot of real
estate, set rents.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:34.830
And all of these are updated
monthly at this time.

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:41.840
So as Matt talked about, where
we see the real opportunity

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:45.170
is some of those heat maps
and digging in, and starting

00:22:45.170 --> 00:22:47.720
to join and analyze.

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:51.230
It used to take us
months to really do this

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:52.610
with whole teams of analysts.

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:54.920
And I was helping buy
a lot of properties

00:22:54.920 --> 00:23:00.320
out of the Lehman crisis
and out of the downturn.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:04.550
And now, it takes
a person a couple

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:07.850
of minutes to really
start joining, analyzing,

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:10.870
identifying opportunities,
identifying risk.

00:23:10.870 --> 00:23:13.310
And for us, that's what's
exciting about this,

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:16.650
is the ability to just
make better decisions much,

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:18.020
much faster than ever before.

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:23.210
And so with that,
here's the URL.

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:26.480
You can go try it out.

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:30.000
And we'd love to get your input.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.173
Thank you very much.

